    "note": "WATCHME(the_heartbeat_outlives_its_caller). Probe ARMED at the berth the ticket names, cairn/devices/ground_loop/probes/does_the_heartbeat_outlive_its_caller.py, module-level PROBE carrying both a carry and an enough. THE PREDICATE TAKES NO LIST, and that is the answer to the enumeration problem raised at hypothesize: green is a '.service' (a unit the user manager forked and OWNS) and red is a '.scope' (a cgroup built AROUND processes someone else forked, so its lifetime is the forker's). Every terminal — konsole, vte, tmux, sshd, named or unnamed, and whatever ships next — falls out of the second clause without ever being listed. The watch cannot go stale against a terminal nobody thought of, because it was never asking about terminals. READ LIVE AGAINST TWO PIDS at 16:52 on 2026-08-18: the launcher-started loop 574986 GREEN (cairn-ground-loop.service, lifetime 'manager'), and this session's own shell RED (superclaude-350050.scope, lifetime 'caller') — the negative arm run against a real process in a real terminal scope, not a fixture. THE SMOKE SURFACE WAS DEFECTIVE AND IS FIXED IN THE SAME ACT. Its __main__ accepted a pid argument and IGNORED it: two runs against different pids came back byte-identical, under a heading that read like an answer about the pid asked. That is a diagnostic surface answering a question it was not asked (Law 7), and it is the failure mode that would have made every future hand-check of this watch worthless while looking like it worked. It now honours the argument and echoes it back as pid_asked_about, so the reading names its own subject. AND THE FIRST CLEARANCE DOES NOT COUNT — recorded here because the evidence LOOKS like it should. The live fire drove the real launcher with a stand-in binary that exits immediately, so the spawner (574929) was gone within seconds and outlived_its_spawner came back True against a loop only 70 seconds old. That was the FIXTURE satisfying 'enough', not a caller dying. In production the spawner pid IS the claude session, via exec, and it lives for hours. The ticket's spec already ruled on this in advance — 'NOT satisfied by the launch that installs it... Enough when it has seen at least one caller die while the loop kept beating' — and the probe's docstring had claimed a DIFFERENT reason for standing (that the live loop was placed in its unit by hand), which the live fire made false the moment the launcher started the loop itself. The docstring now says the spec's reason. THE WATCH STANDS, and what it is waiting for is a real session's death.",
